{
  "id": "long-haul",
  "name": "The Long Haul",
  "description": "The Long Haul, a 24-hour hauler stop built into a Spine pylon at deck level, up a service ramp drivers use and nobody else is supposed to. A long cream counter with chrome-edged stools, a flat-top griddle, two steel coffee urns, a pie case with a few sad slices, a kitchen pass with a warm light, three green-shaded pendant lamps. An old jukebox glowing amber by the door, a coat rack with a trucker's cap and a hi-vis vest, a mop bucket at the end of the counter. A long run of windows onto the deck: parked heavy haulers with their marker lights on, sodium lamps, the highway curving off on its legs and the core's towers beyond. A pink neon cup glows in the window. Oxblood booths along the glass, each with a little lamp; the corner booth on the right is the private one. A checkered floor, bad coffee, real eggs, and drivers who never look up.",
